nodecode-ambassly is a rewrite of MelMayssonOwen's MelMayssonOwen/hermes-plugins:

  https://github.com/MelMayssonOwen/hermes-plugins
  commit f203c3f897784d731ef48bc0974104a4d08cf5f8
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Opsibyte (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's `ambassly` package -- `mcp.json`'s one streamable-HTTP
server at https://ambassly.com/api/mcp, keyed by a Bearer scoped key, and the
`ambassly-affiliate-program` skill's playbook -- as one Nodecode cell. The
cell speaks that server's own protocol (initialize, notifications/initialized,
tools/list, tools/call over JSON-RPC, reading a JSON or an event-stream
answer) instead of needing an MCP client beside it, so every tool the server
offers is still reachable, under the same key and at the same endpoint, and
the key is sent there and nowhere else. The skill's playbook is the text of
(help :ambassly), unchanged in substance: pick the merchant side or the
creator's side first, confirm a program's values before creating it, treat
each commission as a person's decision, one link per piece of content, report
the numbers returned and never estimate a missing one, and keep keys and
Stripe signing secrets out of messages.

Left out, and why: the plugin's `plugin.json` extension block that told Hermes
to ask before a write-capable call. Nodecode has no per-server trust flag, so
the cell carries the same posture in two ways instead -- the tool description
says which calls are writes and that a commission is the person's decision,
and the playbook asks for an explicit answer before approve_commission,
reject_commission, create_program or add_stripe_webhook_secret. Also left
out: the server's per-tool input schemas, which are still served and still
read (the cell hands the model the catalog at run time) but are not projected
onto two Nodecode tools, because the tool list is the server's and changes
there, not here. The Claude Code and Cursor snippets in
`references/setup.md` belong to other clients and have no counterpart here.
